# fstab for the mt6877 platform (common to all mt6877 devices).
#
# Reconstruction of the realme 11 Pro 5G stock fstab, reduced to what is true of
# the platform. Sources: the live A15 device's /vendor/etc/fstab.mt6877, the
# live mount table, and the EROFS odm/etc/oplus.fstab from the stock A13 OTA.
#
# WHAT IS PLATFORM vs WHAT IS DEVICE
#   platform: the logical partition layout (system/vendor/product/odm/system_ext)
#             and the first-stage mount flags.
#   device:   partition SIZES, the super group name, the AVB pubkey path, and
#             any device-specific mount (nvdata, protect1/2, persist...).
# Those device-specific lines are deliberately NOT here. Copy the device's own
# fstab in, do not inherit one.
#
# Two realme specifics that are NOT platform facts and are therefore absent:
#   * the my_* regional logical partitions (my_company, my_region, my_heytap,
#     my_carrier, ...) -- an OPPO/realme regional split, not a chipset trait
#   * /mnt/vendor/protect1, protect2, nvdata, nvcfg -- realme security storage

system        /system        ext4  ro,barrier=1  wait,logical,first_stage_mount
vendor        /vendor        ext4  ro,barrier=1  wait,logical,first_stage_mount
product       /product       ext4  ro,barrier=1  wait,logical,first_stage_mount
system_ext    /system_ext    ext4  ro,barrier=1  wait,logical,first_stage_mount
odm           /odm           ext4  ro,barrier=1  wait,logical,first_stage_mount

# The stock images are EROFS on BOTH A13 and A15 (verified: on the live A15
# device /vendor, /product, /system_ext and /odm all mount as `erofs`, and the
# system partition is erofs on /). The platform ships ext4 lines because that is
# what an AOSP-built super uses; a device that mounts the stock EROFS partitions
# instead overrides this file with its own.
#
# The stock fstab (/vendor/etc/fstab.mt6877) lists BOTH variants per partition,
# erofs first then ext4, with the flags:
#   system     wait,slotselect,avb=vbmeta_system,logical,first_stage_mount,
#              avb_keys=/avb/q-gsi.avbpubkey:/avb/r-gsi.avbpubkey:/avb/s-gsi.avbpubkey
#   vendor     wait,slotselect,avb,logical,first_stage_mount
#   product    wait,slotselect,avb,logical,first_stage_mount
#   system_ext wait,slotselect,avb,logical,first_stage_mount
#   odm        wait,slotselect,logical,first_stage_mount,
#              avb_keys=/vendor/etc/oplus_avb.pubkey
#
# NOTE for anyone testing a GSI: the system line REFERENCES GSI AVB keys
# (/avb/{q,r,s}-gsi.avbpubkey) but they are NOT PRESENT. Verified by extracting
# both ramdisks: the vendor ramdisk holds only first_stage_ramdisk/fstab.mt6877
# and lib/modules/*.ko (225 modules), and the init_boot ramdisk holds only the
# GKI generic ramdisk plus Magisk's overlay.d/sbin/*.xz. There is no /avb
# directory in either. So a GSI will NOT satisfy AVB through those keys -- the
# line is MTK fstab boilerplate for a key set this build does not ship.
#
# The odm key IS real: /vendor/etc/oplus_avb.pubkey exists (520 bytes).
#
# Practical consequence: a GSI test here needs verification disabled
# (fastboot --disable-verification) or a GSI signed with a key the device
# actually trusts. The unlocked bootloader makes that possible, but do not
# expect the stock AVB chain to accept an arbitrary GSI.
#
# The 225 modules in vendor_boot/lib/modules are the other thing worth knowing:
# on this platform the kernel modules ship INSIDE vendor_boot, not as a separate
# partition, which is why CONFIG_MODULES matters for any kernel rebuild.
#
# /data is f2fs (measured on the live device), /metadata is ext4.

# Device-specific mount points. Each device tree supplies these; they are listed
# here only as a checklist of what a device fstab is likely to need, NOT as
# active lines:
#   /dev/block/by-name/metadata    /metadata
#   /dev/block/by-name/userdata     /data         (f2fs on this device)
#   /dev/block/by-name/nvdata       /mnt/vendor/nvdata
#   /dev/block/by-name/protect1     /mnt/vendor/protect_f
#   /dev/block/by-name/protect2     /mnt/vendor/protect_s
#   /dev/block/by-name/persist      /mnt/vendor/persist